.TH KP-SYNC-CONFIG 5 "2026-11-04" "kp-sync 0.3.1" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
kp-sync-config \- TOML configuration for kp-sync
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I ~/.config/kp-sync/config.toml
.br
.I ~/.config/kp-sync/NAME.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR kp-sync (1)
reads the default configuration from
.IR ~/.config/kp-sync/config.toml .
If that file does not exist, built-in defaults are used, unless other
profiles exist in that directory: then
.BR kp-sync (1)
stops and asks for
.BR \-\-profile .
The
.B kp-sync \-\-profile NAME
option selects
.IR ~/.config/kp-sync/NAME.toml ,
and an explicit
.B \-\-config FILE
selects another TOML file; both must exist. Profiles use the same schema;
they do not migrate or copy databases or history.
.PP
Unknown keys, unknown tables and values of the wrong type are rejected
with an error naming the key. Durations are integer seconds unless stated
otherwise; negative durations are rejected. Paths may start with
.B ~
for the home directory. Command settings are TOML arrays of strings, run
directly without a shell, never shell command strings. Keep secrets out of
command arguments and configuration files: passwords are cached only in
the kernel keyring.
.PP
Each setting below shows its type and default. A minimal configuration
for a machine with a runtime hub, a cloud copy and key drives is:
.PP
.nf
[[endpoints]]
name = "cloud"
type = "rclone"
remote = "GoogleDrive:kp"

[[endpoints]]
name = "pendrive"
type = "file"
source = "runtime"
.fi
.SH TOP-LEVEL SETTINGS
.TP
.BR hub " (path)"
The local copy that applications open and the only place where merging
happens. Default
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pass.kdbx ;
named profiles default to
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/PROFILE/pass.kdbx .
A hub in
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
(memory-backed, cleared at logout) is rebuilt from the other copies when
needed and may be removed by
.B kp-sync down
once its content is safe elsewhere.
If
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset, this setting is required.
.TP
.BR hub_persistent " (boolean, default false)"
.B true
declares that
.B hub
lives on permanent storage (normally an encrypted disk), for a machine that
keeps its own copy, such as a second laptop that works offline. kp-sync
then never deletes the hub, and
.B [daemon].idle_sync
defaults to true. With
.BR false ,
the hub is treated as a disposable runtime copy.
.TP
.BR require_encrypted " (boolean, default: value of hub_persistent)"
When the hub is persistent, refuse to use it unless it is on a
dm-crypt/LUKS device (checked with
.BR findmnt (8)
and
.BR lsblk (8))
or on a ZFS dataset with native encryption whose key is loaded (checked
with
.BR "zfs get encryption,keystatus" ).
Set
.B false
only for a hub on storage encrypted by other means.
.TP
.BR host " (string, default: the system host name)"
Name of this machine in the history; the hub is recorded as the copy
.BR hub:HOST .
Must be unique among machines sharing a history and stay stable.
.TP
.BR db_name " (string, default \(dqpass.kdbx\(dq)"
Database file name used on every copy: on the cloud remote, in the drive
directory and in sessions. Must be a single file name, not a path.
.TP
.BR key_file " (path, default ~/data/Personal/pass.keyx)"
KeePass key file used together with the password to open the database for
merges, content checks and restores. It is never copied to permanent
storage.
.SH ENDPOINTS
Each
.B [[endpoints]]
table describes one copy of the database besides the hub. Any number of
endpoints, including none, may be configured. Drives and sessions that
appear at runtime are added automatically.
.SS "Common keys"
.TP
.BR name " (string, required)"
Unique name, starting with a letter or digit and containing only letters,
digits,
.B _
and
.BR \- .
Used in messages,
.B status
and
.BR [baseline].stores .
.TP
.BR type " (string, required)"
.RS
.TP
.B file
A database at a local path, or the removable-drive slot
.RB ( "source = \(dqruntime\(dq" ).
.TP
.B rclone
A cloud remote reached with
.BR rclone (1).
.TP
.B command
Any other storage reached with user-supplied fetch/store commands.
.RE
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default true)"
.B false
keeps the table in the file but ignores the endpoint.
.TP
.BR readonly " (boolean, default false)"
.B true
makes the copy a source of information only: it is never written. A change
found in it is not promoted; it is kept as a side copy in the runtime
backups and reported once per distinct content. A read-only endpoint
cannot be a baseline store.
.TP
.BR optional " (boolean, default false)"
.B true
means the copy is often absent, and that is normal: when it cannot be
reached nothing is logged and it does not cause exit status 4.
.TP
.BR on_unavailable " (string, default \(dqwarn\(dq)"
What to do when the endpoint cannot be reached during a
synchronization:
.RS
.TP
.B warn
Log an
.B INFO:
line, synchronize the remaining copies, and report the endpoint as
unreachable (exit status 4).
.TP
.B skip
As
.BR warn ,
without the log line.
.TP
.B fail
Abort the whole synchronization with an error (exit status 1); nothing is
written.
.RE
.IP
In every case the endpoint is retried on the next run; nothing is queued.
.TP
.BR timeout " (number, default 30)"
Seconds allowed for each fetch, store or probe of this endpoint; must be
greater than zero. A command that exceeds it counts as unreachable.
.TP
.BR foreign_writers " (boolean, default true for rclone, false otherwise)"
.B true
means programs other than kp-sync may edit this copy (phone apps on a
cloud copy, a read-write remote session). A change found there gets the
.I foreign change
check controlled by
.BR [verify].foreign_check ,
which protects against an app saving an outdated in-memory database.
.TP
.BR identity " (string)"
Stable name of the physical copy in the history. Defaults:
.BI file: NAME
for a fixed file,
.BI cloud: REMOTE
for rclone,
.BI command: NAME
for a command endpoint. Machines that share a copy under different
endpoint names must use the same identity. Ignored for runtime drives,
whose identity is always the drive's
.IR SERIAL:FS_UUID .
.SS "file endpoints"
.TP
.BR path " (path)"
A fixed database file, for example on a mounted network share. Required
unless
.B source
is set.
.TP
.BR source " (string)"
Only
.B \(dqruntime\(dq
is accepted: this endpoint is the template for removable drives registered
at runtime with
.BR "kp-sync attach" ,
.B up
or
.BR drive-sync .
Each attached drive becomes a separate copy, identified by
.IR SERIAL:FS_UUID ,
whose database is
.IR MOUNTPOINT/[drives].directory/db_name .
Cannot be combined with
.BR path .
At most one enabled runtime file endpoint is allowed.
.PP
Writable file endpoints are updated with an atomic rename exchange, or on
filesystems without it (exFAT) with a displace-then-install sequence; a
save that races with the update is kept as a backup and merged on the next
run.
.SS "rclone endpoints"
.TP
.BR remote " (string, required)"
rclone remote, optionally with a folder:
.B \(dqGoogleDrive\(dq
or
.BR \(dqGoogleDrive:kp\(dq .
The database is
.IR REMOTE:db_name
and the shared history is
.IR REMOTE:[baseline].dir .
.TP
.BR rclone_config " (path)"
rclone configuration file provided by another tool (for example copied
from a key drive). kp-sync only reads it and runs rclone with its own
private per-profile copy; while no copy exists the endpoint reports its
credentials as unavailable. Without this key rclone uses its usual
configuration file.
.TP
.BR download ", " upload " (array of strings)"
Replace the transfer commands. Defaults:
.B [\(dqrclone\(dq, \(dqcopyto\(dq, \(dq{remote}\(dq, \(dq{file}\(dq]
and
.BR [\(dqrclone\(dq,\ \(dqcopyto\(dq,\ \(dq{file}\(dq,\ \(dq{remote}\(dq] .
.B {remote}
is replaced by the remote database path and
.B {file}
by the local file; each command must contain
.BR {file} .
.B copyto
updates the file in place, which keeps the cloud provider's version
history.
.TP
.BR probe " (array of strings)"
Replace the cheap change check, which must not use
.BR {file} .
By default kp-sync observes the database and the history with one
.B rclone lsjson \-\-hash
listing per run. A listed content hash of at least 64 bits (MD5, SHA-1,
SHA-256, Dropbox's content hash, ...) identifies a known version without
a download; remotes without such a hash are downloaded every run. A custom
probe disables the combined listing.
.TP
.BR rclone_transport " (string, default \(dqauto\(dq)"
.RS
.TP
.B cli
Run a separate
.B rclone
process for each operation.
.TP
.B auto
Let the daemon keep one
.B rclone rcd
server per endpoint on a private socket and send operations to it, which
avoids rclone start-up time. Falls back to separate processes while the
server is not answering, for custom commands, and with rclone older than
1.61.
.RE
.SS "command endpoints"
.TP
.BR fetch " (array of strings, required)"
Command that writes the database to the local path
.BR {file} ,
which must appear in it.
.TP
.BR store " (array of strings, required)"
Command that uploads the local file
.BR {file} ,
which must appear in it.
.TP
.BR probe " (array of strings)"
Optional command that prints a non-empty token describing the current
remote copy on standard output; it must not use
.BR {file} .
.TP
.BR probe_is_content_hash " (boolean, default false)"
.B true
declares that the
.B probe
output is a content hash: it changes whenever the content changes.
An unchanged output is then taken as the recorded version without a
download. Requires
.BR probe .
With
.BR false ,
the copy is fetched on every run.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
[[endpoints]]
name = "nas"
type = "command"
fetch = ["scp", "nas:kp/pass.kdbx", "{file}"]
store = ["scp", "{file}", "nas:kp/pass.kdbx"]
probe = ["ssh", "nas", "sha256sum", "kp/pass.kdbx"]
probe_is_content_hash = true
.fi
.SH [baseline]
The shared history (versions, their parents and which copy holds which
version) is stored in
.I baseline stores
so that machines agree on what changed.
.TP
.BR stores " (array of strings, default [\(dqcloud\(dq, \(dqpendrive\(dq])"
Endpoint names, in priority order, that hold the shared history. Only
enabled, writable
.B rclone
and runtime-drive
.B file
endpoints qualify. With the default, names that are not configured are
ignored silently. An explicit list must contain at least one usable store;
unusable names are reported as warnings. Writable drives keep history too,
so a machine started offline can still recognize their copies. Names are
local to each configuration, but the same physical copy needs the same
identity on every machine.
.TP
.BR dir " (string, default \(dqkp-sync\(dq)"
History directory, relative to the root of every store (the rclone remote
or the drive's mount point). Machines sharing a store must use the same
value. Changing it does not migrate existing history.
.TP
.BR keep_days " (integer, default 180)"
Age in days after which unused versions and copy records not seen since
are pruned. Ancestry needed by retained versions is kept.
.SH [drives]
.TP
.BR notify_stale " (boolean, default true)"
Show a desktop notification when an attached drive held an outdated copy
and was updated.
.TP
.BR directory " (string, default \(dq.\(dq)"
Database directory relative to a drive's mount point. Absolute paths and
.B ..
are rejected. It does not move the drive's history directory or rescue
files, which stay at the drive root.
.TP
.B [drives.aliases]
Optional display names, keyed by the complete
.B SERIAL:FS_UUID
identity. Values must be non-empty and free of control characters. Aliases
only change what
.B status
shows (the technical identity is still shown below it); they do not
affect synchronization, mount matching or drive selection. Duplicate names
are allowed.
.PP
.nf
[drives.aliases]
"1111222233334444:6FD1\-7B31" = "Daily backup"
.fi
.SH [verify]
Checks applied to a candidate before it is written anywhere.
.TP
.BR max_entry_drop " (number 0..1, default 0.10)"
.TQ
.BR min_entry_drop " (integer, default 5)"
A candidate that has lost more than
.B max_entry_drop
(as a fraction) of the entries of the version it came from, and also more
than
.B min_entry_drop
entries, needs explicit confirmation. Both limits must be exceeded, so
small databases are not blocked by a single deletion.
.TP
.BR foreign_check " (string, default \(dqalways\(dq)"
How changes found on
.B foreign_writers
endpoints are checked against an application that saved an outdated
copy:
.RS
.TP
.B always
Check every foreign change; this needs the password. A save from a stale
cache contributes only entries modified after its recorded version.
.TP
.B merge-only
Check only when another copy changed as well. A lone phone edit is then
promoted without asking for the password, accepting the risk that it was
saved from a stale cache.
.RE
.SH [merge]
.TP
.BR engine " (string, default \(dqkp-sync\(dq)"
.RS
.TP
.B kp-sync
Built-in 3-way merge, field by field, against the common ancestor version.
Independent changes merge without questions; conflicting edits of the same
field go to review; deletions are respected.
.TP
.B keepassxc
Use
.BR "keepassxc-cli merge" :
newest modification wins and there is no review. With KeePassXC 2.6.6 a
deletion recorded in the other copy was not applied, so deleted entries
can come back.
.RE
.TP
.BR review " (string, default \(dqinteractive\(dq)"
How conflicts found by the built-in merge are decided:
.RS
.TP
.B interactive
Show the field-by-field conflict review (secrets masked) on the terminal or
desktop.
.TP
.B auto-hub
Resolve every conflict with the hub's value, without asking.
.TP
.B auto-other
Resolve every conflict with the other copy's value, without asking.
.TP
.B defer
Do not merge; leave all copies as they are and record a pending decision
for
.BR "kp-sync resolve" .
.RE
.SH [ui]
.TP
.BR mode " (string, default \(dqauto\(dq)"
.RS
.TP
.B auto
Terminal if standard input is a terminal, otherwise desktop dialogs if a
display is available, otherwise
.BR none .
.TP
.B tty
Always ask on the terminal of the waiting client.
.TP
.B gui
Always ask with desktop dialogs.
.TP
.B none
Never ask; questions become pending decisions.
.RE
.IP
The
.B \-\-ui
command-line option overrides it.
.TP
.BR gui " (string or array of strings, default \(dqzenity\(dq)"
Desktop dialog backend:
.RS
.TP
.B zenity
GNOME/GTK dialogs.
.TP
.B kdialog
KDE dialogs.
.TP
.I other
Any other string is an executable, and an array is a full argument list,
for a custom program that reads a JSON request on standard input and
writes a JSON answer on standard output.
.RE
.IP
On the desktop, the field-by-field conflict review uses a built-in
tkinter window with every backend.
.TP
.BR ask_first " (boolean, default true)"
Before asking for the password or opening the conflict review, first ask
whether to do it now (for example
.IR "Merge now" / Later ),
so a background synchronization does not interrupt the user unexpectedly.
.TP
.BR dialog_timeout " (integer, default 600)"
Seconds to wait for an answer before taking the safe answer (defer,
overwrite nothing).
.TP
.BR notify " (boolean, default true)"
Show desktop notifications (copy updated, endpoint unavailable, reopen
needed, ...).
.TP
.BR progress " (boolean, default true)"
Show a progress window when a desktop synchronization takes longer than
two seconds. It names the profile and hub; its
.B Hide
button closes only the window, never the synchronization. Not shown for
background polls.
.SH [credentials]
.TP
.BR cache_timeout " (integer, default 900)"
Seconds the main password stays cached in the kernel keyring as
.BR kp-sync:PROFILE:password .
.B kp-sync forget
removes it earlier.
.SS [credentials.keepassxc]
.TP
.BR url " (string)"
Configuring this table opts into retrieving the password from an unlocked
KeePassXC database through the browser-integration protocol, before the
manual cache and the password prompt, whenever an operation needs
credentials. The URL is only a lookup key matching one entry and is never
fetched; use a unique host name per profile, for example
.BR https://kp-sync-default.invalid .
Requires the optional
.B keepassxc
package extra and browser integration enabled in KeePassXC. The first
interactive use asks KeePassXC for an association. Several matching
entries, a locked database or an unavailable KeePassXC fall back to the
normal prompt with a warning. Silent polls skip the lookup so they never
trigger approval dialogs. Retrieved passwords are validated against the
database and never cached across operations. The configured key file is
still required.
.PP
Only pairing metadata persists under
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/kp-sync/
(default
.IR ~/.local/state/kp-sync/ ),
isolated by host and profile and pinned to the database identity. No
password is stored there.
.B kp-sync keepassxc-disconnect
removes the local pairing; revoke the connection in KeePassXC as well.
.B kp-sync forget
clears only the keyring cache.
.SH [backups]
.TP
.BR keep " (integer, default 20, at least 1)"
Number of timestamped runtime backups to keep. A backup is made before
every overwrite of a copy. Backups live in the runtime directory and are
lost at logout or reboot.
.SH [daemon]
.TP
.BR debounce " (number, default 2)"
Seconds to wait after a hub save before synchronizing, so a burst of
writes leads to one run.
.TP
.BR poll_interval " (integer, default 900)"
Seconds between endpoint checks while a lease is held, mainly to bring in
cloud edits made on phones.
.B 0
disables polling.
.TP
.BR idle_exit " (integer, default 120)"
Seconds the daemon stays running after the last lease ends, so a quick
relaunch does not restart it.
.TP
.BR prestart_timeout " (integer, default 20)"
Seconds
.B kp-sync run
waits for the pre-launch synchronization before starting the application
on the existing hub. Without a hub, the launch waits for the first
download regardless.
.TP
.BR access_lease_ttl " (integer, default 3600)"
Seconds a kp-access lease (BEGIN..END) keeps the daemon running without any
operation. Expiry only lets the daemon exit; the staged copy stays until
END and the next operation takes the lease again.
.TP
.BR hub_path_unit " (boolean, default true)"
Install and enable a systemd path unit that starts the daemon when the hub
is saved by a program kp-sync did not start, so that save is synchronized
too.
.TP
.BR idle_sync " (boolean, default: value of hub_persistent)"
Install and enable a systemd timer that runs one synchronization at login
and every
.B idle_sync_interval
without holding a lease, so a persistent hub stays current for later
offline use. Normally off for a runtime hub, which usually does not exist
while nothing uses it.
.TP
.BR idle_sync_interval " (integer, default 3600)"
Seconds between idle synchronizations.
.TP
.BR rescue_wait " (integer, default 60)"
At shutdown, when the runtime hub holds content that exists on no other
copy, seconds to wait for the user to insert a writable drive that will
receive a rescue file.
.PP
Changes to
.B hub_path_unit
and
.B idle_sync
take effect after
.B kp-sync service install daemon
and
.BR "kp-sync service enable daemon" .
.SH [sync]
.TP
.BR lock_timeout " (integer, default 60)"
Seconds a
.B \-\-no\-daemon
run waits for the engine lock held by another run before failing.
.TP
.BR max_restarts " (integer, default 3)"
How many times a synchronization starts over when a copy changes while it
is running (detected by the re-check before each write) before it gives up
and reports the problem.
.SH [launch]
.TP
.BR default_app " (string, default \(dqkeepassxc\(dq)"
Application started by
.B kp-sync run
and
.B up
without
.BR \-\-app :
.BR keepassxc ,
.B keepass-legacy
or the name of an
.B [apps.NAME]
table.
.TP
.BR confirm_without_cloud " (boolean, default false)"
When the pre-launch synchronization could not reach every endpoint, ask
before starting the application on a hub that may be outdated. With
.B false
the application starts and a notification is shown.
.TP
.BR quit_on_last_drive " (boolean, default true)"
When the last attached drive is removed
.RB ( down ,
or
.B eject
with no other drive), quit the applications started by
.B up
and remove the runtime hub after its final synchronization. Set
.B false
on machines where drives are only extra copies.
.SH [apps.NAME]
Defines an application for
.B kp-sync run \-\-app NAME
or
.BR [launch].default_app .
.I NAME
uses letters, digits,
.B _
and
.BR \- .
A table named
.B keepassxc
or
.B keepass-legacy
replaces the built-in definition entirely; keys it omits take the
defaults below, not the built-in values.
.TP
.BR command " (array of strings)"
Program and arguments to start. Arguments given to
.B kp-sync run
are appended. For applications named
.B keepassxc
or
.B keepass-legacy
started without extra arguments, the hub path is appended; for other
names include the hub path in
.B command
if the program needs it.
.TP
.BR process " (array of strings)"
Process names used to find the application when it was started without
kp-sync, so the daemon can still hold a lease for it. KeePassXC is also
found through its D-Bus name.
.TP
.BR reloads " (boolean, default false)"
.B true
declares that the application reloads the database by itself when the
file changes on disk. With
.BR false ,
after kp-sync updates the hub the user is told to reopen it, and until the
application reopens the file its saves are treated as foreign changes, so
an old in-memory copy cannot revert the update. For KeePassXC the daemon
also checks its
.I Automatically reload the database when modified externally
setting and treats it as not reloading if the setting is off.
.TP
.BR single_instance " (boolean, default false)"
.B true
declares that only one instance runs at a time: a new
.B run
activates the existing window instead of starting another, or passes its
extra arguments to the running instance.
.PP
Built-in definitions:
.B keepassxc
runs
.BR keepassxc ,
reloads, single instance;
.B keepass-legacy
runs
.BR keepass ,
does not reload, single instance.
.SH SESSIONS
.SS [sessions]
Shared settings for
.B kp-sync session
and named SSH sessions.
.TP
.BR enabled " (boolean, default false)"
Allow remote sessions at all.
.TP
.BR mount_dir " (string, default \(dq~/kp\(dq)"
Remote directory where the database is mounted with sshfs.
.TP
.BR tunnel_port " (integer 1..65535, default 2222)"
Port on the remote host for the reverse tunnel to the local sshd. Only one
session per remote host can use a port at a time; a port already in use
there fails the session with an error.
.TP
.BR default_mode " (string, default \(dqro\(dq)"
.B ro
mounts the database read-only on the remote side;
.B rw
lets the remote side save, and those saves are synchronized back as
foreign changes.
.TP
.BR session_key_ttl " (integer, default 86400)"
Lifetime of a session's SSH key: the
.B expiry-time
written on its
.I authorized_keys
line. The key is removed earlier when the session ends.
.SS [ssh_sessions.NAME]
A named session managed with
.BR "kp-sync service start|stop|status ssh-session NAME" .
.I NAME
has up to 48 letters, digits,
.B _
and
.BR \- .
.PP
.nf
[ssh_sessions.office]
target = "user@host:~/kp"
mode = "rw"
mount_dir = "~/kp"
unconfined = false
.fi
.TP
.BR target " (string, required)"
.BR [USER@]HOST[:PATH]
destination. A path in the target overrides
.BR mount_dir .
.TP
.BR mode " (string)"
.B ro
or
.BR rw ,
as in
.BR [sessions].default_mode ,
which is the default.
.TP
.BR mount_dir " (string)"
Remote mount path. Defaults to
.BR [sessions].mount_dir .
.TP
.BR unconfined " (boolean, default false)"
.B true
serves the session without
.BR bwrap (1)
confinement. Should be avoided.
.PP
The session runs in a transient systemd user service, survives closing the
terminal, and holds the SSH access and synchronization lease until it is
stopped. It does not survive logout or reboot unless the systemd user
manager lingers. Logs are in the user journal.
.SH KP-ACCESS
.SS [access]
Settings for
.B kp-sync access-op
as the
.B \-\-file\-op\-cmd
of
.BR kp-access .
.TP
.BR db_path " (string)"
The database path as kp-access shows it to clients (its
.BR \-\-path\-name ),
for example
.BR /kp/pass.kdbx .
Must be a normalized absolute path. A directory component is preferred
because it narrows the operations the server allows; a root-level path
such as
.B /pass.kdbx
also works. Required for
.BR access-op .
.TP
.BR staging_dir " (string, default \(dqstaging\(dq)"
Directory holding the copy staged for kp-access. A relative path is
relative to the profile's runtime directory; an absolute path must still
be inside it and must not contain symbolic links.
.SS [access_owners.NAME]
A named
.B kp-access-owner
process managed with
.BR "kp-sync service start|stop|status access-owner NAME" .
.TP
.BR command " (array of strings, required)"
Program and arguments, run directly without a shell inside a transient
systemd user service. It must stay in the foreground. Its standard output
and error are discarded; kp-sync lifecycle messages and errors go to the
user journal. Use an absolute executable path if the program is not in the
systemd user manager's
.BR PATH .
.PP
.nf
[access_owners.windows]
command = ["/path/to/venv/bin/kp-access-owner", "\-\-config", "/path/to/owner.toml"]
.fi
.PP
The owner must still be configured to use
.B kp-sync access-op
as its file-operation hook when synchronization integration is desired.
.SH EXAMPLE
A second laptop with a persistent hub on an encrypted disk:
.PP
.nf
hub = "~/data/pass.kdbx"
hub_persistent = true
key_file = "~/data/pass.keyx"

[[endpoints]]
name = "cloud"
type = "rclone"
remote = "GoogleDrive"

[[endpoints]]
name = "pendrive"
type = "file"
source = "runtime"

[launch]
quit_on_last_drive = false
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kp-sync (1),
.BR rclone (1),
.BR keepassxc-cli (1),
.BR systemd-run (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
